hey guys Pete here today I'm going to do  one last better call Saul bonus video as  we wait for the finale of season 5 it's  time to talk about Eduardo Lalo  Salamanca as we approach the end of  better call Saul season 5 Lalo has  become the most interesting villain in  the current story and this has led some  fans to declare that he's the best  villain the Breaking Bad universe has  ever produced the hype is definitely  real Lalo is a great character and a  great villain officially I think it's  too early to tell where a hill ranked  against so many other great villains  this franchise has introduced us to but  I think now is a great time to look at  what makes him so compelling to borrow a  quote from the man himself when he first  meets Jimmy he tells him you're the guy  for this in many ways I believe Lalo is  the guy for this and that's what I want  to explore before we dive in this is  your spoiler warning this video will  talk about everything that's happened in  better call Saul through season 5  episode 9 and everything that happened  in Breaking Bad if you haven't made it  that far then this video will be filled  with massive spoilers that you won't be  able to unsee Lalo Salamanca is a late  comer to the origin story of Saul  Goodman his first appearance doesn't  happen until the 8th episode of season 4  he comes to Albuquerque to help with the  Salamanca family business which has  experienced some setbacks in season 2  his cousin  Tuco Salamanca is sent to prison after  nacho Varga hires Mike to set him up in  season 3 his uncle Hector Salamanca  becomes permanently disabled after he's  poisoned by nacho the cousins  Leonel and Marco Salamanca come to visit  their uncle after a stroke but end up  having to flee back south of the border  to Mexico after attacking the Espinosa  gang their departure leaves nacho who by  this time is under Gus Frings influence  in charge of the Salamanca family  operations in New Mexico that opens the  door for a la lo to arrive and get  things in order for the Salamanca's  prequel shows are hard to make because  the end of the story there based on has  already been told  better call Saul has always excelled  in light of that it never ceases to  amaze me how they come up with ways to  make the journey to that end compelling  and full of surprises the show has  always been impressive in its ability to  take things that seem minor from  Breaking Bad and turn them into  something interesting there have been so  many little details and throwaway lines  from Breaking Bad that the writers have  picked up and run with Lalo Salamanca is  one of the best examples of that  the name Lalo first comes up in Breaking  Bad season 2 episode 8 which shares the  title better call Saul with our current  series the episode is best remembered as  the introduction of Saul Goodman but it  also serves as the origin of both nacho  Varga and Lalo Salamanca in that episode  Jesse and Walt disguised in ski masks  kidnaps Saul Goodman and take him to a  freshly dug shallow grave in a remote  part of the desert we watch as Saul  finds himself fearing for his life and  his first instinct is that the  kidnappers are members of the cartel as  you would expect Saul tries to talk his  way out of the situation first he tries  to shift the blame saying no it wasn't  me it was ignacio he's the one then in  Spanish he says he's a friend of the  cartel and then he asked them Lalo  didn't send you know Lalo so from that  exchange two characters from Saul's  backstory are born Ignacio aka nacho and  of course Lalo neither character is  mentioned by name again in Breaking Bad  but it is implied later in a  conversation between Gus and Hector that  Lalo is no longer alive in season 4  episode 11 Gus goes to visit Hector at  Casa tranquila after killing off Don  Eladio and the other leaders of the  cartel he tells Hector that his grandson  Joaquin was killed as well and that  since he was the only family he had left  to Salamanca family name will die with  Hector in his business dealings and  personal obsession with the family Gus  would be better informed on whether Lalo  was still around and Saul Saul is  clearly afraid of him and believes he's  still alive in that moment  when they're in the desert but based on  what Gus said to Hector  we know Lal those days are numbered he  could even die during better call Sol's  timeline just as long as it happens in a  way that Jimmy doesn't know about it and  that's not a very big stretch  considering when people commit murder  they usually try to keep it a secret  while all of this is intriguing enough  in a show that has no shortage of crazy  and/or violent criminals  what makes Lalo stand out from the crowd  through the first four seasons of better  call Saul and the Prix Lalo days the bad  guys in the show had been contained to  one side of the show or the other we had  the Jimmy McGill's law career side of  the show where his brother Chuck was the  main villain on that side Jimmy and Kim  battle with the number of people like  Howard Hamlin and the kettlemans  and then over on the other side we had  the cartel story where Mike and nacho  had to deal with characters like Tuco  and Gus the two stories didn't really  crossover outside of Jimmy and Tuco's  introduction in the first couple of  episodes in season one at the end of  season five we've reached the point  where the show's two sides of the story  have finally come together the legal  side of Kim and Jimmy has collided with  the cartel side of Mike Gus and nacho as  these things come together we need a  villain that can be effective in both  worlds and Lolo seems to be the right  man for the job  Lalo is the guy for this from his first  introduction we can see that he is the  most charismatic Salamanca we've ever  met in that way it's easy to make the  argument that he is the best villain  that better call Saul has produced Chuck  was a great character and the family  dynamic made him a cruel opponent for  Jimmy but he never would have been able  to operate in the cartels world Lalo on  the other hand can sit down with Gus and  Juan bolsa as a representative for his  family but you could also imagine him  being able to strike up a conversation  with Howard Hamlin the other Salamanca  family members we've met our a mixed bag  of crazy and ruthless Tuco which is the  first of the family we met in  bad was a drug-using maniac erratic to  his core the cousins had a laid-back way  in comparison but they seemed to truly  enjoy bringing violence into a situation  when they thought it was necessary  Hector was also erratic and always  appeared to have a chip on his shoulder  that brought things down around him  until he ended up confined to that  wheelchair Lalo is loyal to his family  which is something we know about all the  Salamanca's but he's also clearly  different that's not to say he doesn't  share in some of the family's dark  tendencies he killed Fred from travel  wire without a second thought and the  story he shared with Hector about  burning down a hotel after torturing its  owner was disturbing he's violent and  remorseless but he also seems like he's  got things under control  he's smart and resourceful and we've  seen him stack up as a worthy adversary  for Gus  we've seen them be patient in that area  and methodical to a certain extent he  can also be chatty and friendly and has  the ability to charm people when he  entered the story in season 4  I immediately perked up at the idea of  him breathing life into the cartel side  of the story as interesting as learning  more about Mike and Gus can be that side  of the story started lagging behind  because the stakes just aren't the same  considering we know how their stories  end Lalo presented a real problem for  Gus which got things moving in the right  direction and then once he met Saul we  could see the two stories starting to  come together that's where he gets  really fascinating from my perspective  his charisma and ability to win people  over bears a lot of resemblance to what  makes Jimmy so effective Jimmy / Saul  can do the things he does because even  though he's comfortable getting down in  the dirt with criminals  he still has a straight life that exists  outside the criminal world he has a  criminal nature he commits criminal acts  but he toes the line and his law license  gives him a sense of respectability Lalo  is almost the exact opposite he lives a  criminal existence but can navigate the  other side of the story and even excel  there creating a care  in Lalo that shares many of the same  strengths as Saul creates the perfect  storm Saul can usually talk his way out  of any situation he finds himself in  when Lalo shows up at his door in 509  things are instantly turned upside down  Jimmy isn't able to talk himself out of  this one Lalo once again appears to be  the guy for this he sees right through  Jimmy's lies leaving him defenseless  until the only thing that saves him is  Kim stepping in she's able to use her  skills to neutralize the situation and  in the end he decides to leave we  haven't really met a character who is so  perfectly suited to go up against Jimmy  McGill and as we approach the end game  of Jimmy's transition into Saul Lalo  could really prove to be an important  piece in that puzzle for now we don't  know how long we'll land but either way  he's proven to be one of the most  captivating and infectious characters  that we've come across in the Breaking  Bad universe in short order he brought  excitement into Gus and Mike's side of  the story  delivered some extra humor and more  tension in his quest to make Gus's life  difficult  he became the perfect bridge to bring  both sides of the story together and  emerged as the perfect adversary for  Saul Goodman all at the same time he's  an awful human being and yet you almost  want a root for the guy not knowing how  his story ends keeps things exciting we  expect him to die but there also needs  to be a setup for Jimmy to still be  terrified of him four years from now he  could die in the season 5 finale or he  could die off-screen years later after  the events of Breaking Bad season 2 it  adds an extra dimension and I'm excited  to see where they take it as I said  before I think it's a little early to  put him up at the top of the list of  course right now he's the most exciting  villain in the Breaking Bad universe but  we'll have to see where his story ends  it's the same kind of thing that I think  about when people say which is better  Breaking Bad or better call Saul they're  both different shows and one of them is  complete there's things I like better  about both series but I really wouldn't  pick one over the other until I know how  better call Saul ends so I'm going to  leave it there let me know what you  think about Lalo Salamanca in the  comments how do you think his story will  end as far as coming into the story late  he stacks up pretty well in that  category and Breaking Bad we had Uncle  Jack and Todd they did the job  Todd especially was a pretty good  character but they couldn't really live  up to Gus Lalo came to the game a little  bit later and that gives him an  advantage so drop a comment to tell me  what you think please like this video if  you enjoyed it please subscribe to my  channel if you haven't already thanks  for watching I'll talk to you soon
 

